<WikiLayout title="RE:Heads, Please! FAQ - Frequently Asked Questions" description="Answers to frequently asked questions about RE:Heads, Please! codes, values, guides and wiki information.">
  <article class="page-shell"><h1>RE:Heads, Please! FAQ</h1><p class="page-intro">Quick answers and direct links to the wiki’s most useful resources.</p>
    <p>This page helps readers choose the correct RE:Heads, Please! Wiki section before searching through a longer guide or database list. Each answer points to a focused page type. The Wiki keeps codes, entity records, progression instructions, comparisons, and update notes separate so one URL does not have to answer several unrelated questions.</p>
    <h2>Where are the latest codes?</h2><p>Visit the <a href="/codes">Codes hub</a> for working-status pages, redemption steps, expiration context, and troubleshooting. A code should not be treated as active merely because it appears in an older list or video. Use the current redemption response and the page’s recorded review context, and do not assume a reward when the result has not been verified.</p>
    <h2>Where can I compare values?</h2><p>The <a href="/database/value-list">Value List</a> organizes the value fields that are present in the verified item, coin, and accessory datasets. Value is not the same as rarity, availability, usefulness, or player preference. Open the individual entity page for its acquisition note, category, update date, and other documented fields before making a comparison.</p>
    <h2>How do I find an item, coin, or accessory?</h2><p>Start with the <a href="/database">Database</a> when you know the category but not the exact page. Items, Coins, and Accessories each have their own hub and entity URLs. Searching the exact displayed name produces a clearer result than using a broad description, and the related-entry links can continue the lookup without combining separate entities.</p>
    <h2>Which guide should a new player read?</h2><p>The <a href="/guides/beginner-guide">Beginner Guide</a> provides the starting framework. Farming, progression, controls, and game-mechanics pages answer narrower process questions. Choose the guide that matches the current goal, check visible requirements in the live game, and move to a database record when the question changes from a process to a named entity.</p>
    <h2>Is the wiki official?</h2><p>No. This is an independent community resource and is not affiliated with Roblox or the game’s developers. It does not replace the current in-game interface. Verification badges and dates describe the Wiki’s record state, while changing availability, requirements, values, and mechanics should be checked against the latest in-game information.</p>
    <h2>What should I do when information is missing?</h2><p>Treat a missing field as unknown. Do not fill it with a guessed probability, cost, reward, location, or permanent rule. Use the closest relevant page, record the exact wording shown by the game, and return after the claim can be verified under clear conditions.</p>
